At the risk of using the "M" word (Moria) in this group, let me go on
record as saying that Moria does a much better job on this issue than
our beloved nethack.  To summarize: A player has "mana" points, similar to
the magic points in nethack, that are required to cast spells.  These
are like hit points in that they recover with time.  In Moria, it is
necessary to have the spellbook or prayer book that contains the spell
or prayer desired - but these books contain multiple spells and are 
not used up like nethack spellbooks.

(An aside: Prayers are like spells, except that different characters
(priests/paladins) use them.)

Spells may be cast as many times as mana will allow - the spell never
gets "overused".  What keeps this whole thing in balance is that the
really good spells take a fair amount of mana, and mana doesn't regenerate
too quickly.  Further balance is added by the fact that only wizards/priests
can cast the highest spells/prayers (and only when they are very high level).
Other characters with greater fighting abilities (rogues, rangers, paladins)
can do some spells/prayers, while the toughest fighters cannot use magic
at all!

Now Moria has an implicit design that is oriented toward archieving the
ends differently, based on the character type.  It is very hard to "max
out" your stats - much harder than in nethack.  Thus magic using characters
end up depending on their magic as intended, and never become great 
hack and slash types.

Some time ago, there was a lively discussion in this newsgroup about
whether nethack should do the same thing - limit the fighting abilities of
wizards and give them more magic using powers (for example).  I do not
wish to restart that argument and I readily point out that nethack is n-o-t
Moria.  On the other hand, lets not be too proud to borrow some good ideas.

OK, here's the target for the flaming epistles:

1) Having spells get "used up" is dumb.  Irrespective of previous arguments
   about nethack "realism", it just doesn't make "sense".  On the other
   hand, spells taking up "mana" or magical energy or whatever seems "right".

2) Game balance should be maintained by making the real good stuff take
   enough magical energy that a player cannot cast those spells frequently.

3) Successful casting of a spell should be related to intelligence and the
   difficulty of the spell (as it is now), but once a spell has been cast
   once, a fudge factor toward success (for experience!) should be added.
   Note that the spell is still as "expensive" in magical energy.

4) Lets keep nethack "equal opportunity" by relating the spell casting
   ability only to intelligence and not give Wizards any more head start
   than their natural intelligence gives them.   After all, if they eat
   enough spinach so that they can fight like a Barbarian, the barbarian
   should be able to quaff enough blessed potions of enlightenment to
   cast spells with the mages.

5) Some really powerful spells could still be "one-shot" deals.  Let
   those come from the player's deity.  "You have been granted a single
   spell of genocide".

6) I think nethack's way of giving out spells - via disposable spellbooks,
   is better than the Moria way.   Low level spells might also be granted
   by sitting on a throne ("A new spell occurs to you").

Looking over the above, it's not too radical a change.  Mainly just not
letting the spells wear out and balancing that by making the magical
energy points mean something.


